## Reached at
  A Go HTTP service inside the container, one listener bound to 0.0.0.0:8080;
  callers reach it at http://127.0.0.1:8080 and ws://127.0.0.1:8080:
      server := web.New("0.0.0.0:8080")
  The service is the Go program staged by the image build as /app/main.go
  (package main, module `app` per /app/go.mod, go 1.17), compiled and started by
  the container entrypoint, working directory /app:
      cd /app && go run main.go
  run inside a loop that restarts the process whenever it exits. Because
  `go run` compiles at every start, the running process is always built from the
  current tree under /app after a restart; there is no separately installed
  binary of this service anywhere in the image.

  Both routes are registered through the web framework github.com/ecnepsnai/web
  v1.5.1, resolved from the vendored copy under /app/vendor, with one shared
  options value carrying an AuthenticateMethod:
      server.Socket("/ws/admin", func(request web.Request, conn web.WSConn) { ... }, options)
      server.API.GET("/api/admin", func(request web.Request) (interface{}, *web.Error) { ... }, options)
      options := web.HandleOptions{AuthenticateMethod: authenticate}

  Ownership: everything compiled into the server process — /app/main.go,
  /app/go.mod, and the vendored module copies under /app/vendor — is
  project-owned surface. Preexisting/system items are only the Go toolchain and
  the loopback network. The container health check also requests
  GET /api/admin on the same listener, confirming the route identity.

## Must provide
  listener on 0.0.0.0:8080   one HTTP listener serving both routes below
  GET /api/admin             HTTP API route, registered via server.API.GET with
                             the shared web.HandleOptions
  /ws/admin                  WebSocket route on the same listener, registered via
                             server.Socket with the same web.HandleOptions;
                             callers initiate the WebSocket upgrade handshake
                             against this path
  Authorization              HTTP request header carried by caller requests on
                             both routes; the existing wiring supplies an
                             AuthenticateMethod in web.HandleOptions for both
  WebSocket text frames      each frame a caller sends is a JSON-encoded object
                             with the keys "action" and "data"

## Called as
  HTTP route:
      requests.get("http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/admin",
                   headers={"Authorization": <string>}, timeout=10)
      Authorization — request header; a string, passed in the form
      "Bearer <token>"; callers also send the request with no Authorization
      header and with other string values.

  WebSocket route, handshake:
      create_connection("ws://127.0.0.1:8080/ws/admin",
                        header=[<string>, ...], timeout=10)
      header — a list, possibly empty, of "Name: value" header strings; when
      Authorization is present it is a string, passed in the form
      "Bearer <token>", and callers also pass other string values and an empty
      value.

  WebSocket route, after the handshake:
      ws.send(json.dumps({"action": <string>, "data": <string>}))
      action — object field; a string
      data   — object field; a string
  Callers send one such frame, then read one frame, repeating per action, and
  close the connection when done.

## Setup required
  The server process must be running and listening on 0.0.0.0:8080 before calls
  arrive; callers poll GET /api/admin in a loop until it answers. Changes to
  sources under /app are picked up only after a process restart, because the
  entrypoint loop re-runs `go run main.go`, which recompiles at each start.
  Module resolution is vendor mode: with go 1.17 and /app/vendor present,
  github.com/ecnepsnai/web and its transitive dependencies resolve from the
  vendored copies there, so the vendored copy is what is compiled into the
  process and nothing is fetched at startup.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest process inside the same container that makes the
  GET requests with the `requests` HTTP client and performs the WebSocket
  handshakes and frames with the `websocket-client` library, all against
  127.0.0.1:8080. A shell runner starts the suite: it reinstalls pinned
  versions of pytest, requests, and websocket-client from PyPI, unsets proxy
  and PYTHONPATH environment variables so observations go directly to the
  listener, waits for the HTTP route to answer, then runs pytest. The runner,
  the client libraries, and the readiness poll belong to the calling side and
  are not part of the implementation; the engineer does not rebuild them. The
  entrypoint restart loop is image infrastructure, not application code.
