## Setup required
  The PHP interpreter at /usr/local/bin/php must be present, and the driver
  script /tests/harness.php must exist and be readable. The application tree
  /app/src must contain the three class files at the paths in "Reached at",
  and every *.php file under /app/src is require_once'"'"'d at process start;
  a missing required file or an uncaught Throwable during load aborts the
  driver. The driver supplies framework stand-ins (Project, ProjectManager,
  PFUser, UserManager, URLVerification, EventManager, ProjectCreationData,
  CompanyTemplate, ProjectPostRepresentation, the TuleapTemplate subclasses,
  and the exception classes) only when the application tree has not already
  declared the same class — so a class a fix adds under /app/src is honoured
  rather than clobbered. Entry-point construction by reflection requires
  every declared constructor parameter to be resolvable by its declared type;
  the mapping from a declared type to a stand-in instance is fixed in the
  driver.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner invokes the driver script through `subprocess.run([PHP,
  HARNESS, ...])`, capturing stdout/stderr and parsing exactly one JSON
  object from the last non-empty line of stdout. The driver script, the
  PHP interpreter invocation, the recursive autoload of /app/src, the
  reflection-based constructor resolution, and every framework stand-in
  class named above belong to the calling side — not to the implementation.
  The engineer does not rebuild the driver or the stand-ins; only the three
  class files under /app/src are project-owned. The scenario world (four
  fixed projects with fixed access) and the per-scenario access policy are
  set by the driver before each entry point is called.
