This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A CommonJS package placed at /app by the image build; the retained
  package.json names it `macaddress` and declares `"main": "index.js"`.
  Callers run the absolute Node binary /usr/local/bin/node on a driver
  script and load the modules by absolute path:
      var macaddress = require('"'"'/app/index.js'"'"');
      var backend = require('"'"'/app/lib/linux.js'"'"');
      var backend = require('"'"'/app/lib/unix.js'"'"');
  Before invoking anything, each driver resolves the configured path with
  `fs.realpathSync(require.resolve(...))` and only loads the module when the
  resolved real path begins with `/app/`, so the call can only land on code
  inside the application tree. Two independent driver processes reach the
  implementation through exactly these paths and entry points.
  Inside index.js, a switch on `os.platform()` binds the backend used by the
  exported functions: on `'"'"'linux'"'"'` it is `require('"'"'./lib/linux.js'"'"')`,
  resolved relative to /app/index.js. `one` and `all` flow the caller'"'"'s
  first argument into that backend function. The package also retains a
  small self-driver (`npm test` runs `node test.js`) that requires `./index`
  and invokes the same three exports.

## Must provide
  /app/index.js          CommonJS module; module.exports carries:
    one                  function, two call forms (see Called as)
    all                  function, callback-based
    networkInterfaces    function, takes no arguments, used synchronously
  /app/lib/linux.js      module.exports is itself a function
  /app/lib/unix.js       module.exports is itself a function

## Called as
  mod.one(iface, callback)    iface — 1st positional argument; a string
                              callback — 2nd positional argument; a function,
                              which callers declare as function (err, mac)
  mod.one(callback)           single-argument form; the only argument is a
                              function declared as function (err, mac); the
                              interface is chosen by the module itself
  mod.all(callback)           callback — 1st positional argument; a function,
                              which callers declare as function (err, all)
  mod.networkInterfaces()     no arguments; callers use the return value
                              synchronously
  backend(iface, callback)    a platform module required directly is invoked
                              as the exported function itself: iface — 1st
                              positional argument, a string; callback — 2nd
                              positional argument, a function declared as
                              function (err, mac)

## Setup required
  The Node runtime must exist at /usr/local/bin/node (provided by the base
  image). The implementation files must live under /app: the drivers take
  the realpath of what they resolve and do not proceed unless it begins
  with `/app/`. Loading is by absolute path, so no working directory,
  NODE_PATH, or node_modules lookup is involved. The runtime platform is
  Linux, so the dispatch inside index.js selects ./lib/linux.js as the
  backend that serves `one` and `all`.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner writes an inline Node driver script to /tmp, executes
  /usr/local/bin/node on it through subprocess with a sanitized environment
  and a JSON configuration passed as argv[2], then reads a JSON report line
  the driver prints on stdout. The driver performs the module resolution,
  requires the paths above, drives their public exports, and collects what
  the callbacks receive; the calling side also reads interface names and
  addresses from /sys/class/net independently. The runner, the driver
  scripts, the subprocess machinery, and the sysfs reading all belong to the
  calling side, not to the implementation, and must not be rebuilt.
